$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM Solution phase Huisgen azide-alkyne 1,3-cycloaddition of peptide 21 with
phenylacetylene: To peptide 21 (150 μM) dissolved in 50 mM phosphate
buffer (pH 7.2) containing 250 mM NaCl (500 μL) was added CuSO4•5H2O (50
μL of 10 mM stock in H2O, 0.5 μmol, 1 mM final concentration), sodium
ascorbate (5.0 mg, 25 μmol, 50 mM final concentration), phenanthroline
(10 μL of 10 mM stock in DMSO, 100 nmol, 200 μM final concentration), and
phenylacetylene (3.0 μL, 27 μmol, 54 mM final concentration)
consecutively. The reaction mixture was stirred gently for 60 min at 37
°C to obtain peptide 109. The crude reaction mixture was then filtered
(0.45 μm Nylon syringe filter) and extracted with diethyl ether and
CH2Cl2 to remove excess reagents. The modified peptide was purified via
reverse phase analytical HPLC using 20 min isocratic 100% buffer A,
followed by a linear gradient of 0-50% buffer B in buffer A over 60
minutes. Peptide 109. Expected mass 677.3, observed mass (M + Na) 700.2,
tR = 55.7 min.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):NOTES
$DATUM Huisgen coupling, phosphate/NaCl buffered solution
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):TITLE
$DATUM Proline Editing: A General and Practical Approach to the Synthesis of
Functionally and Structurally Diverse Peptides. Analysis of Steric versus
Stereoelectronic Effects of 4-Substituted Prolines on Conformation within
Peptides
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):AUTHOR
